================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-cartography - map projections, resonance-terrain contours, and
                             multi-agent cartographic overlays for SAR fleets

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital cartography defines:
           • resonance-time map projections
           • resonance-terrain contouring
           • harmonic-phase and sweep-ancestry overlays
           • multi-agent cartographic fusion
           • constellation-scale temporal terrain modeling

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-cartography describes how RTT transforms orbital timing,
       resonance envelopes, harmonic cycles, and sweep ancestry into navigable
       temporal terrains. These terrains behave like topographic maps: they
       have ridges, basins, gradients, and contours that guide sweep placement,
       Δv scheduling, and constellation-scale choreography.

       Cartography enables:
           • multi-resolution temporal visualization
           • resonance-aware navigation
           • harmonic-phase alignment
           • constellation-scale map overlays
           • predictive terrain evolution across cycles

       WRSADC uses cartographic maps to guide planning and anomaly recovery.

MAP PROJECTIONS
       Map projections convert resonance-time data into 2D or 3D coordinate
       systems for analysis and planning.

       Projection types:

           linear projection
                  Maps time directly to a horizontal axis.
                  → simplest for sweep scheduling.

           polar-phase projection
                  Maps harmonic phase φ_harm to angular coordinates.
                  → ideal for harmonic-lock visualization.

           resonance-height projection
                  Maps envelope amplitude R(t) to vertical height.
                  → creates a “resonance terrain.”

           spectral-density projection
                  Maps S(f,t) to color or intensity.
                  → highlights spectral fragmentation.

           hybrid projection
                  Combines resonance height + harmonic angle + timing grids.
                  → full RTT terrain.

       Projection selection depends on mission goals.

EXAMPLE: RESONANCE-TERRAIN PROJECTION
       Envelope amplitude R(t) over one orbital period T:

           Peaks at: 12, 54, 78
           Valleys at: 30, 90

       Projection:
           • peaks become “mountain ridges”
           • valleys become “basins”
           • sweep windows placed on ridge crests
           • Δv triads placed in basins

       Result:
           • intuitive terrain for sweep planning
           • stable resonance-aware navigation

RESONANCE-TERRAIN CONTOURS
       Contours represent lines of equal resonance amplitude, spectral density,
       or harmonic-phase alignment.

       Contour types:

           amplitude contours
                  Lines of constant R(t).
                  → identify resonance plateaus.

           spectral contours
                  Lines of constant S(f,t).
                  → identify spectral stability zones.

           gradient contours
                  Lines of constant gradient polarity G(t).
                  → identify polarity transitions.

           harmonic-phase contours
                  Lines of constant φ_harm.
                  → identify harmonic-lock corridors.

       Contours reveal the “shape” of resonance-time terrain.

EXAMPLE: CONTOUR MAP
       Over one orbital period T:

           R(t) = high at 10–20, 50–60
           R(t) = medium at 20–40
           R(t) = low at 0–10, 40–50, 60–T

       Contours:
           • high-density ridge: 10–20, 50–60
           • medium plateau: 20–40
           • low basins: 0–10, 40–50, 60–96

       Sweep placement:
           • sweeps at 15 and 55
           • Δv triads at 30 and 80

MULTI-AGENT CARTOGRAPHIC OVERLAYS
       Overlays combine maps from multiple platforms into a unified
       constellation terrain.

       Overlay types:

           synchronous overlay
                  All platforms share identical contours.

           harmonic overlay
                  Maps aligned via harmonic ratios.

           resonance overlay
                  Maps aligned via envelope peaks.

           ancestry overlay
                  Maps aligned via sweep lineage.

           mesh overlay
                  Distributed overlay across constellation nodes.

       Overlays reveal multi-agent resonance corridors.

EXAMPLE: MULTI-AGENT OVERLAY
       Platforms A, B, C:

           A resonance peaks: 12, 54
           B resonance peaks: 20, 60
           C resonance peaks: 15, 50

       Overlay terrain:
           • shared ridge at ~15
           • shared ridge at ~55
           • shared basin at ~30

       Result:
           • sweeps at 15 and 55
           • Δv triads at 30
           • constellation sync at 0 and 45

CARTOGRAPHIC FUSION
       Cartographic fusion merges projections, contours, and overlays into a
       unified resonance-time terrain.

       Fusion layers:

           1. timing-grid projection
           2. resonance-terrain projection
           3. harmonic-phase overlay
           4. ancestry contouring
           5. constellation overlay

       Fusion output:

           • sweep corridors
           • Δv corridors
           • resonance ridges
           • harmonic valleys
           • sync plateaus

       Fusion is recomputed each triad cycle.

CARTOGRAPHIC FAILURE MODES
       Cartography may degrade due to:

           • projection distortion
           • contour collapse
           • overlay misalignment
           • resonance-terrain fragmentation
           • constellation map mismatch

       Diagnostic indicators:

           cartography-integrity score
           projection error
           contour deviation
           overlay drift
           sweep-terrain mismatch

       WRSADC halts unsafe cartographic states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • map projections
           • contour evolution
           • overlay history
           • fused terrain maps
           • constellation cartography evolution

       Lineage enables long-term terrain forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • generates map projections
           • computes resonance contours
           • overlays multi-agent maps
           • fuses constellation terrains
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe terrain states

       WRSADC is the cartography conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital cartography transforms SAR constellations into navigable
       resonance-time terrains. Projections, contours, and multi-agent overlays
       ensure that sweep choreography, harmonic-lock, and constellation-scale
       synchronization remain stable across long mission timelines.

